<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Kathi people</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Kathis</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Kathi are a community of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kathiawar">Kathiawar</a> peninsula in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gujarat">Gujarat</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>, which is named after them. Pastoralists and warriors who are thought to have entered the region from the north in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-ages">medieval period</a>, they founded several small states, including Jasdan, Jetpur and Bilkha. Their chiefs, known as Kathi Darbars, shared land among branches of the family.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/kathi-people.jpg" alt="Kathi people" width="320" height="511" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Baroda State · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Kathi_man.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The Kāṭhīs are a small group of clans. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maratha-empire">Maratha Empire</a> and later the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/british-raj">British Raj</a> called the region <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kathiawar">Kathiawar</a> after them, as they were prominent there in the 17th and 18th centuries. Once under the Sūmrās of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sind">Sind</a>, they fled <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sindh">Sindh</a> and took refuge with the Vālā chieftain in Saurashtra; they are documented serving under the Cūdāsamās in the eleventh century. The Vālā leader was reportedly cast out of his <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caste">caste</a> for dining with them and deposed by his brothers, and he then joined the Kāṭhīs to conquer lands, among them the Than-Chotila area taken from the Soḍhās.</p><p>Tradition tells of a Kāṭhī named Vāloji who defeated Jām Abdā of Thān with the help of the Sun god and repaired the sun temple on Kandolā Hill. His daughter Sonabāi became its priestess, and her descendants, the Bhagats, form the main sun-worshipping strand of the Kāṭhīs.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The Kāṭhīs were known as fighters, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cattle">cattle</a> rustlers and breeders of hardy horses, and were often hired as military aides without rising to the standing of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rajputs">Rajputs</a>. By the fifteenth century some branches had settled in Kachchh and founded the kingdom of Pavāgaḍh near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bhuj">Bhuj</a>, until the Jāḍejās drove them to the Than region. Originally pastoralists, many raided villages until the 19th century; by the 20th most had settled on farmland. They are now classed as an Other Backward Class in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gujarat">Gujarat</a>'s central list.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Organisation and way of life</h2><p>The main branches include the Vaḷā, Sakhāyat, Khumān, Khācar and Auratiya, some formed by marriages between Rājput men and Kāṭhī women; groups of mixed descent rank higher than those of purely Kāṭhī ancestry. A ruler's land was divided among his sons, and though the British urged <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/primogeniture">primogeniture</a>, few Kāṭhī rulers had adopted it by the late 1920s. They have taken many <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rajputs">Rajput</a> customs, including the seclusion of women. Once sun-worshippers alone, they now also worship <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hindu</a> deities and western Indian goddesses, and the Swaminarayana movement has drawn followers among them.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Kathi people” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Bilkha was formed around 1760 as a share of the lands of the Vala <b>Kathis</b>, who also ruled Jetpur State.</blockquote><a href="/state/bilkha-state">Bilkha State</a></li><li><blockquote>Jasdan was held by the Khachar clan of <b>Kathis</b>, a pastoral warrior people who gave Kathiawar its name.</blockquote><a href="/state/jasdan-state">Jasdan State</a></li><li><blockquote>Jetpur came under the Vala branch of the <b>Kathis</b>, a pastoral warrior community that gave its name to Kathiawar, around 1760.</blockquote><a href="/state/jetpur-state">Jetpur State</a></li></ul></section>
